## Definition

Assume full AC. For an even-order integral matrix $V$ that is the Seifert matrix of a knot, **S-equivalence** means the equivalence relation generated by integral unimodular congruences $V\mapsto P^TVP$ with $\det P=\pm1$, where $x,y\in\mathbb Z^n$ and $z\in\mathbb Z$, and the two enlargements
$$E_0(V;x,y,z)=\begin{pmatrix}V&x&0\\y^T&z&0\\0^T&1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad E_1(V;x,y,z)=\begin{pmatrix}V&x&0\\y^T&z&1\\0^T&0&0\end{pmatrix},$$
and their reverses. For any oriented link $L$, define its **Conway polynomial** $\nabla_L(z)\in\mathbb Z[z]$ as the normalized invariant characterized by $\nabla_U(z)=1$ for the unknot $U$ and
$$\nabla_{L_+}(z)-\nabla_{L_-}(z)=z\,\nabla_{L_0}(z)$$
whenever the three oriented links agree outside one crossing ball and inside it are respectively the positive crossing, negative crossing, and orientation-compatible smoothing; positive means $\det_P(v_{\rm over},v_{\rm under})>0$, where $v_{\rm over},v_{\rm under}$ are the projected oriented tangent vectors and $P$ is oriented so the viewing normal followed by a positive basis of $P$ is ambient-positive. The smoothing reconnects the four oriented half-arcs in the unique noncrossing way compatible with their directions. The Seifert-determinant lemma proves existence, uniqueness, and the determinant formula. For a knot, let $\Delta_K^\#(t)$ be the unique associate of its Alexander polynomial satisfying $\Delta_K^\#(1)=1$ and $\Delta_K^\#(t^{-1})=\Delta_K^\#(t)$. Set $z=t^{1/2}-t^{-1/2}$; the convention is $\Delta_K^\#(t)=\nabla_K(z)$, with $t^{1/2}$ a formal Laurent variable.

